--TEST--
Core\IO::temporaryDir asks for `fs.write` on the path it is about to create, so a grant covering the working directory does not reach the temporary root — the member creates a name the program never wrote down and is still not exempt from the grant — ADR 0118 §§ 1-2, spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The bound this case names is the one that would be easy to get wrong in the
// other direction: `fs.write` *is* granted here, generously, for the directory
// the program is running in. A member that read "is fs.write granted at all"
// rather than "is it granted for this path" would pass every other assertion
// in the suite and hand out a directory under a root the operator never named.
Core\IO::write("proof.txt", "the grant covers this\n");
echo "working directory: writable\n";
Core\IO::remove("proof.txt");

// Same capability, different path, and the answer has to change.
try {
    string $made = Core\IO::temporaryDir();
    echo "temporary root: writable\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    // The path in the message is the host's own temporary root, so what is
    // asserted is the half an operator acts on: which capability, and for
    // which member.
    if (Core\Str::contains($refused->message, "Core\\IO::temporaryDir needs the capability `fs.write` for ")) {
        echo "temporary root: refused, naming fs.write\n";
    } else {
        echo "temporary root: refused, saying something else\n";
    }
}

// And a refusal leaves nothing behind, which is the ordering claim: the name
// is chosen, then asked about, then created -- never created and then checked.
if (Core\IO::exists(".")) {
    echo "working directory: still there\n";
} else {
    echo "working directory: gone\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
working directory: writable
temporary root: refused, naming fs.write
working directory: still there
